gNJ(){return"About this app"},
ga_c(){return"Privacy policy"},
ga_b(){return"App homepage"},
gZd(){return"Getting started with CrispNote"},
gZf(){return"Welcome"},
gZe(){return"Welcome to CrispNote!\nFeel free to delete this guide once you've read it\n(deleted notes stay in the trash for 7 days and can be restored).\n\nCrispNote stores your data in your own Google Drive or OneDrive. There is no server of ours anywhere in the picture, and the developer cannot access your data.\n\nThe next pages cover how syncing works, what else the app can do, and how to bring notes over from another app.\nTo turn the page, use the row below the text: tap an arrow or a dot, or drag the row sideways. You can also pick a page from the contents button at the top right, and on a computer the arrows at the edge of the screen work too."},
gZh(){return"Sync and backup"},
gZg(){return"\xb7 Everything works offline. Changes are sent up the next time a sync succeeds.\n\xb7 Sign in with the same account on another device to use the same notes there.\n\xb7 You can change where you sync, or sign in again, from Settings at any time. Using the app with no backup at all, on this device only, is also fine."},
gZj(){return"Things you can do"},
gZi(){return'\xb7 Lock: protect the whole app, or individual notes, with a PIN or biometrics (in Settings).\n\xb7 Reminders: set a date and time on a note and get a notification (not shown on desktop, which has no notifications).\n\xb7 Multiple pages: one note can hold several pages, like this one. Reorder them from the contents at the top right.\n\xb7 Images and files: attach images or any file to a note. There is an example attached at the bottom of this page.\n\xb7 Search: the magnifying glass in the list searches across every note and checklist.\n\xb7 Formatting: headings, bold, bullets and more can shape the body. **The next page, "Styling your text", shows exactly what to type.**\n\xb7 On a computer: right-click a card for its menu. Ctrl+F searches, Ctrl+N makes a note, Ctrl+Shift+N makes a checklist, and Esc closes. Drag a checklist into empty space to park it in the column you want.'},
gZn(){return"Styling your text"},
gZm(){return'Add a few symbols as you type and the body is styled when you read it. **What gets saved is exactly what you typed**, so exports still open cleanly in other apps.\n\nTap the pencil button for **edit mode** and a row of formatting buttons appears under the body. **Select some text and press one to wrap it; press again to remove it.** For the line styles (heading, bullets, numbers, tick boxes), just put the cursor on the line and press.\n\nYou can also type them by hand. The grey boxes below show what to type.\n- `**bold**` gives you **bold**\n- `*italic*` gives you *italic*\n- `~~strikethrough~~` gives you ~~strikethrough~~\n- Start a line with `# Heading` for a heading\n- Start a line with `- Bullet` for a bulleted list\n- Start a line with `1. Numbered` for a numbered list\n- Start a line with `> Quote` for a quote\n- `- [ ] To do` gives you a tick box\n- A line of just `---` becomes a horizontal rule\n- `[[Note name]]` becomes a link to that note\n\n## Tags, for finding things later\nIn edit mode a "+" appears under the page name. That is where you add **tags**, and search can narrow the list down to any tag you have used. **A `#` typed into the body does not become a tag** \u2014 it is the same symbol as the heading above, so it only ever means one thing.\n\n## Can\'t see what was typed?\nSwitch to edit mode: the styling drops away and the raw symbols appear. **Open this very page in edit mode and you can see exactly how the lines above were written.** If you\'d rather not have styling at all, turn it off under Settings \u2192 Display \u2192 "Text formatting".'},
gZl(){return"Bringing notes in and out"},
gZk(){return"Notes you made in another app can come with you. Everything below lives under \"Data\" in Settings.\n\n\xb7 Formats you can import: Google Keep (Takeout), Evernote (.enex), text and Markdown (including Notion exports), and CrispNote's own format.\n\xb7 **Importing twice doesn't duplicate anything.** When the same note already exists, the newer version wins \u2014 so if an import goes wrong, just do it again.\n\xb7 Two export formats: CrispNote and Markdown. The CrispNote format includes your attachments, which makes it the one to use for backups or for moving to another device.\n\nIf you like having a copy of your own alongside cloud sync, exporting now and then is a good habit."},
gZu(){return"My first checklist"},
gZv(){return"Tap an item to tick it off"},
gZw(){return'Use "Add an item" to add more'},
gZx(){return"Ticked items collect at the bottom automatically"},
